WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.392
Stage 0: the evaluation set, before any code.

2
00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:06.851
Write at least 30 questions before you build the pipeline.

3
00:00:07.131 --> 00:00:18.245
For each one, record the question as a user would phrase it, the gold evidence (which document and section answers it, plus the exact quote that proves it), and a short reference answer.

4
00:00:18.525 --> 00:00:31.261
Barnett and colleagues’ lesson that RAG can only be fully validated in operation is not an excuse to skip this; it’s a reason to start with the best approximation you can make and grow it from real traffic later.

5
00:00:31.541 --> 00:00:34.229
Make the set hard in the ways real users are.

6
00:00:34.509 --> 00:00:51.981
A useful mix for 30 questions: about 18 answerable from a single passage, about 5 that need two or more passages combined, at least 4 that the corpus cannot answer (so abstention is measured), and a few traps: questions phrased in different words than the documents use,

7
00:00:52.261 --> 00:00:56.016
and questions where an older document disagrees with a newer one.

8
00:00:56.296 --> 00:01:00.840
If your corpus is the PEPs, “what replaced PEP X’s proposal?

9
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.643
” is a good trap, because superseded PEPs are still in the repository.

10
00:01:05.923 --> 00:01:08.824
Label evidence at a level your chunker can’t break.

11
00:01:09.104 --> 00:01:14.331
If gold labels are chunk ids and you change the chunk size, every label is wrong.

12
00:01:14.611 --> 00:01:22.504
Label the document, the section, and the evidence quote instead, and count a retrieved chunk as a hit if it contains the evidence.

13
00:01:22.784 --> 00:01:29.035
Then you can compare chunking strategies on the same set, which is one of the most valuable experiments you’ll run.

14
00:01:29.315 --> 00:01:33.645
Keep a handful of questions aside and don’t look at their results while you tune.

15
00:01:33.925 --> 00:01:38.533
Thirty questions is small, and it’s easy to overfit a prompt to the ones you stare at.

16
00:01:38.813 --> 00:01:40.925
Studio 3 makes this split formal.
